    "summary": "Epic The Other One jam, soaring guitar, strong acoustic set",
    "blurb": "The performance is anchored by a half‑hour odyssey of The Other One and a fiery Cosmic Charlie. Acoustic highlights include Friend Of The Devil, Dire Wolf and Candyman, while the band’s tight interplay drives the electric second set.",
    "review": "On May 2, 1970 at Harpur College the Dead delivered one of the spring ’70 tour’s most memorable nights.  The first set rolls out a breezy acoustic groove—Friend Of The Devil, Dire Wolf, Candyman and Cumberland Blues glide into an intimate New Riders cameo before the band launches into a soaring electric second set.  The centerpiece is a monumental half‑hour stretch of The Other One that builds from a tight drum duet into a cosmic crescendo, punctuated by a blistering Cosmic Charlie and a playful Cryptical Envelopment sandwich.  A solid acoustic finish with Morning Dew, Viola Lee Blues and the gentle “And We Bid You Good Night” rounds out a show that feels both relaxed and incandescent.\n\nAcross all five soundboard‑derived sources the playing is described as “locked in,” with Garcia’s guitar soaring on both acoustic numbers and the electric marathon, Phil’s bass punching through on Cumberland Blues, and the dual drummers providing an unshakable pulse.  While a few recordings note occasional reel‑flip gaps or mono‑only sections, the overall audio remains clear enough to capture the band’s dynamic flow and the rare NRPS guest spot.  For anyone looking to hear the Dead at a creative peak in ’70, this Harpur College show is essential listening.",
